{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":"s (two "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" arrays for the preimage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex)."}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","kind":"typeIdentifier","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition"}]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"text":"Discussion","level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A counting sort of f gives the preimage rows and the in-degrees. Peeling the vertices of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in-degree zero, repeatedly, visits the tree vertices leaves first, summing subtree sizes on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the way, and leaves exactly the cyclic ones; each cycle is then walked from its least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex. Revisiting the peeled vertices in reverse, parents first, gives every tail length","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and root and a preorder numbering of the trees from the subtree sizes; a counting sort by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"depth then lays out the level-ancestor table. Nothing recurses or chases a chain of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"dependent reads, so a tail of 10⁷ vertices needs no call stack.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The graph has fewer than 2³¹ vertices.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"note","type":"aside"}],"kind":"content"}],"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"metadata":{"symbolKind":"method","title":"cycleDecomposition()","modules":[{"name":"FunctionalGraphs"}],"remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/FunctionalGraphs\/CycleDecomposition.swift#L269","fileName":"CycleDecomposition.swift"},"roleHeading":"Instance Method","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"FunctionalGraph","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"CycleDecomposition","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV"}],"role":"symbol","extendedModule":"FunctionalGraphs","externalID":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18cycleDecompositionAC05CycleE0VyF"},"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"]]},"kind":"symbol","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"references":{"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/CycleDecomposition":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"CycleDecomposition"}],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition","abstract":[{"text":"Every cycle of a functional graph, and for each vertex its tail and the cycle it reaches, in","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Flajolet and Odlyzko’s terms: walking from ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", the first vertex on a cycle is ","type":"text"},{"code":"root(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"tailLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" steps away (Sage’s “preperiod”), on a cycle of ","type":"text"},{"code":"cycleLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertices. For a permutation, ","type":"text"},{"code":"cycles","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is its cycle decomposition and every tail is zero.","type":"text"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"CycleDecomposition"}],"role":"symbol","type":"topic","title":"FunctionalGraph.CycleDecomposition","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","kind":"symbol"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"title":"FunctionalGraphs","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","kind":"symbol","role":"collection","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","abstract":[],"type":"topic"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"url":"\/documentatio